{
  "configuration_id": "config_006",
  "reason": "With only one completed experiment (config_008: pca_n2000 + resnet50 + avgpool + weighted → 0.639 acc), evidence is very sparse. The most important untested design axis is the RNA encoder: config_008 uses a linear PCA (pca_n2000), which may discard nonlinear structure in the transcriptome. Config_006 swaps only the RNA encoder to scvi_n200 (a learned VAE) while holding all other settings identical (resnet50, avgpool, weighted fusion, multiprofile, multimodal), giving a clean single-variable comparison. This directly reduces uncertainty about whether a learned RNA representation is a meaningful source of accuracy gain. If it helps, it justifies further investment in stronger encoders (scgpt) or more complex aggregation; if not, it tells us the PCA representation is sufficient and we should pivot to image encoder or fusion strategy. Expected relative cost: moderate increase over config_008 (~12–14 GPU hours vs 10.60), since scvi_n200 requires training a small VAE on top of the same image pipeline, adding some overhead but far less than scgpt or ViT-L."
}

